\details{
plnr keys a data function on its arguments without a default. These are
the key fields. Each key field of \code{fn} MUST be \code{scenario}, \code{sim} or a
setting column of \code{CFG$scenarios}. Otherwise \code{\link[=run_plan]{run_plan()}} stops before it
runs anything. plnr calls \code{fn} once for each set of values of
the key fields, and gives each analysis with those values the same data.

\code{add_data()} stops on these arguments of \code{fn}:
\itemize{
\item \code{scenario} or \code{sim} with a default. plnr does not key on it, so every
scenario or simulation would get the same data.
\item \code{sim} without \code{scenario}. Every scenario would get the same data.
}

A data function without \code{scenario} and \code{sim}, for example one that reads a
lookup table, runs once for the whole run and sets no seed.

pbn adds \code{fn} in a wrapper. The wrapper has the key fields of \code{fn} as its
arguments, so plnr keys it in the same way. It calls \code{fn} with them by
name, so another argument of \code{fn} with a default keeps its default. When
\code{sim} is an argument, the wrapper calls \code{set.seed(sim)} before \code{fn}. The
data of simulation \code{k} is thus the same in each run, and does not depend
on the other scenarios of the run. A function that is called with no \code{sim}
sets no seed.

The wrapper needs no object of the R session that adds it. \code{\link[=run_plan]{run_plan()}}
gives each worker the objects that the functions folder defines.

\code{name} MUST be one non-empty string, and it MUST NOT be \code{"hash"}, because
plnr adds \code{data$hash} to the data of each analysis. Two data functions of
one plan MUST NOT have the same name. Add data with \code{add_data()}, not with
\code{p$add_data()}. \code{\link[=save_results]{save_results()}} reads the data functions of \code{add_data()}
only.

While \code{\link[=run_plan]{run_plan()}} runs, an error in \code{fn} fails each analysis that gets
the data. When you call \code{p$get_data()} by hand, the error stops the call.
